\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:analysis}

\paragraph{CV as diagnostic metric.}
Token importance CV correlates with both pruning tolerance and optimal strategy (Table~\ref{tab:scaling_law}). The gap $\Delta$(R$-$L2) decreases monotonically with CV at $k{=}0.5$ and $k{=}0.7$: low-CV models require coverage-preserving methods, while higher-CV models benefit from importance-based scoring. X-VLA illustrates that CV captures effective pipeline complexity beyond connector labels: despite sharing a nominal linear projection with $\pi_0$ (CV$\,{=}\,$0.038), its 12-layer BART encoder yields CV$\,{=}\,$0.151 and threshold-graded behavior. If connector label alone sufficed, X-VLA should behave as uniformly critical like $\pi_0$, but CV correctly differentiates them. At extreme compression ($k{=}0.3$), the relationship partially reverses for $\pi_0.5$ (Appendix~\ref{app:cv_strategy_interaction}); extended analysis in Appendix~\ref{app:cv_extended}.

\paragraph{Re-encoding as general mechanism.}
Both cross-attention and parameter-matched MLP modules achieve comparable recovery ($p = 0.15$), suggesting that learned re-encoding in general, not the attention mechanism specifically, overcomes pixel-shuffle entanglement. The relatively stable M-curve ($M{=}16$: 56.9\% vs.\ $M{=}128$: 58.0\%) with a modest dip at $M{=}96$ (54.5\%, $p{=}0.034$ vs.\ $M{=}64$; statistically significant but within practical tolerance) confirms that token count is secondary once entanglement is broken. The contribution is diagnostic: understanding \emph{why} re-encoding works for pixel-shuffle connectors, rather than proposing a general-purpose compression module. VTR applied to OpenVLA yields 0\% SR even in passthrough mode, confirming specificity to pixel-shuffle entanglement (Appendix~\ref{app:reencoding_analysis}).

\paragraph{Pixel-shuffle sensitivity is empirically novel.}
While the \emph{direction} of pixel-shuffle vulnerability may seem intuitive, neither its \emph{magnitude} (5\% removal $\to$ 0\% SR across all seven tested configurations) nor its consistency across scoring methods, retention ratios, and mechanisms (learned vs.\ random, zero-mask vs.\ true removal vs.\ merging) was established prior to this work. No previous study has empirically tested pixel-shuffle pruning in closed-loop VLA control.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
All closed-loop experiments use the LIBERO simulation benchmark; real-world conditions may alter the compressibility spectrum. The seven models differ in backbone and encoder fusion strategy, preventing clean causal isolation of the connector effect; the monotonic CV-degradation relationship is consistent with the connector being a primary factor but does not constitute causal proof (Appendix~\ref{app:confounders}). $\pi_0$ evaluation is offline only; the $\pi_0$--OpenVLA 63\,pp gap involves differences in connector, backbone, and encoder fusion, limiting causal interpretation. The CV scaling law spans three models and two retention ratios; extension to additional architectures is needed for formal quantitative use.

