\section{Connector Architecture and Token Independence}
\label{app:connector_analysis}
\label{sec:pixel_shuffle}

The seven-model taxonomy (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}) spans a continuous spectrum of token independence structures, each associated with the vision-to-LLM connector rather than model scale or action head design.

Pixel-shuffle connectors create maximal entanglement. SmolVLA's $4 \times 4$ spatial folding packs 16 patches into each token by channel concatenation (CV $= 0.314$, the highest in our study). The folding is fixed and irreversible: removing one token eliminates an entire receptive field, and no downstream layer can recover the discarded content. Tokens encode non-overlapping spatial regions with heterogeneous visual content, making every token simultaneously essential and non-redundant.

Pure linear projections produce the opposite extreme. $\pi_0$ maps each SigLIP patch through a single linear layer with no cross-token interaction, yielding CV $= 0.038$ and near-maximal entropy (0.959). Tokens are genuinely interchangeable: any subset preserves the same per-patch information density as any other subset of equal size.

$\pi_0.5$ reveals a subtlety that $\pi_0$'s offline-only evaluation conceals. The $\times\!\sqrt{d}$ learned scaling preserves near-uniform norms (CV $= 0.055$), yet the closed-loop response is strongly method-dependent: random removal at $k{=}0.5$ retains 69.4\% SR while L2 norm-based removal collapses to 33.4\%. The 36\,pp gap arises because L2 ranking in a low-CV distribution selects spatially clustered tokens whose slight norm variations correlate with image regions, destroying the spatial coverage that this architecture requires. Token position analysis confirms this mechanism: at $k{=}0.5$, L2 selection produces a single dominant cluster containing 100\% of retained tokens with only 78\% spatial coverage, whereas $k{=}0.7$ yields two clusters with 97\% in the largest and 100\% coverage (Appendix~\ref{app:token_position}).

X-VLA's 12-layer BART encoder~\cite{lewis2020bart} introduces cross-token mixing through self-attention, creating partial dependencies that place it between the linear and MLP extremes (CV $= 0.151$, entropy $= 0.614$, the lowest among models). The resulting behavior is threshold-graded: performance holds through $k{=}0.9$ before a sharp cliff at $k{=}0.85$ ($-$31\,pp). The cliff onset is reproducible across three seeds (std $= 0.3$\,pp), confirming it as an architectural property.

MLP projectors (OpenVLA, OFT) apply per-patch non-linear transformations that create partial importance differentiation without cross-token entanglement. OpenVLA's CV (0.215) and OFT's CV (0.219) match within measurement noise, as do their entropy values (0.661 vs.\ 0.667); the connector, not the action head, is associated with token importance structure. CogACT~\cite{li2024cogact} corroborates the graded pattern from the literature (74.2\% SR under 78\% pruning).

Parameter count does not predict pruning tolerance. Ordered by scale: SmolVLA (450M) $<$ X-VLA (880M) $<$ $\pi_0$ (3.3B) $<$ $\pi_0.5$ (4B) $<$ OpenVLA (7B). Ordered by tolerance, SmolVLA remains the minimum, but $\pi_0$ and $\pi_0.5$ at 3.3--4B surpass OpenVLA at 7B, and X-VLA at 880M tolerates more aggressive pruning than OpenVLA. The CV spectrum (0.038 to 0.314) tracks connector complexity rather than parameter count across a 16$\times$ scale range.


\section{CV as Diagnostic Metric: Extended Analysis}
\label{app:cv_extended}
\label{sec:diagnostic}

Token importance CV correlates with both the tolerance class of a model and the optimal pruning strategy, a correlation that emerges from a single mechanistic principle: CV measures the strength of the importance gradient that scoring methods can exploit.

Low-CV models have near-uniform L2 norms. L2 norm ranking in this regime captures spatial artifacts (slight norm variations that correlate with image position) rather than genuine importance differences. Selection based on these artifacts concentrates retained tokens on spatially correlated patches, destroying the uniform coverage that low-CV architectures depend on. Random selection preserves spatial uniformity by construction. The mechanism predicts that the random-over-L2 advantage should grow as CV decreases, and the data in Table~\ref{tab:scaling_law} confirm this at two retention ratios. At $k{=}0.5$: $\Delta = +$36.0\,pp ($\pi_0.5$, CV $= 0.055$) $\to$ $+$26.9\,pp (X-VLA, CV $= 0.151$) $\to$ $-$1.8\,pp (OpenVLA, CV $= 0.215$). At $k{=}0.7$: the same monotonic ordering holds ($+$22.2\,pp $\to$ $+$6.5\,pp $\to$ $-$5.4\,pp). The pattern persists across a 2$\times$ change in absolute token budget, strengthening the interpretation from a coincidence at a single operating point to a structural property of the connector.

High-CV models present a genuine importance gradient where L2 norm ranking aligns with actual information content. OpenVLA (CV $= 0.215$) sits near the crossover: L2 outperforms random by 8.6\,pp at $k{=}0.9$ where the gradient is most exploitable, but the advantage shrinks with decreasing $k$ (5.4\,pp at $k{=}0.7$, 1.8\,pp at $k{=}0.5$) as the proportion of removed tokens exceeds the gradient's discriminative range.

Extreme compression introduces a partial reversal. At $k{=}0.3$, $\pi_0.5$'s L2 selection achieves $53.2 \pm 3.1$\% SR (2 seeds) vs.\ random's 36.4\%. When very few tokens remain, even spatially clustered high-norm tokens provide more signal than a sparse random sample. The V-shaped L2 curve is confirmed across seeds ($\sigma{=}0.1$\,pp at the $k{=}0.5$ valley). The reversal does not invalidate the CV diagnostic; it adds a budget-dependent interaction that future work should characterize across more models.

Attention entropy provides a mechanistic supplement that resolves an apparent paradox. SmolVLA's entropy (0.952) is nearly as high as $\pi_0$'s (0.959), yet SmolVLA collapses under any pruning. SmolVLA's tokens are uniformly \emph{attended} but carry heterogeneous \emph{content} due to pixel-shuffle folding. Entropy captures how the LLM allocates attention across tokens; CV captures the input-side importance structure. Entropy alone cannot distinguish ``interchangeable'' ($\pi_0$) from ``entangled'' (SmolVLA), but CV separates them cleanly (0.038 vs.\ 0.314). OpenVLA's progressive entropy sharpening from 0.98 to 0.48 across layers (Figure~\ref{fig:entropy_layers}) is consistent with a graded regime where the LLM progressively concentrates on higher-importance tokens.


\section{Why Re-Encoding Succeeds: Extended Analysis}
\label{app:reencoding_analysis}
\label{sec:learned_compression}

Learned re-encoding overcomes pixel-shuffle failure because it breaks the rigid spatial entanglement that the $4 \times 4$ folding creates. Cross-attention pooling integrates information across receptive field boundaries, redistributing content from the original 128 tokens into $M$ compressed representations no longer tied to fixed spatial blocks.

The $M{=}128$ passthrough configuration confirms that re-encoding, not compression, is the operative mechanism. Passthrough adds parameters without reducing token count, yet gains 13\,pp over the baseline (44.8\% $\to$ 58.0\%). The relatively stable M-curve ($M{=}16$: 56.9\% closely matches $M{=}128$: 58.0\%, within 1.1\,pp) shows that once entanglement is broken, token count is secondary.

A parameter-matched MLP adapter (22.14M parameters) achieves 63.4\% SR at $M{=}32$, statistically indistinguishable from cross-attention ($p = 0.15$). Re-encoding through any learned mapping that integrates across token boundaries suffices; the attention mechanism itself is not essential.

The remedy is specific to the pathology. VTR applied to OpenVLA yields 0\% SR even in passthrough mode, confirming that re-encoding disrupts rather than assists architectures whose tokens already carry independent per-patch information. VTR succeeds precisely on architectures whose rigid entanglement demands learned redistribution.


\section{Offline Metrics Underestimate Closed-Loop Failure}
\label{app:offline_disconnect}
\label{sec:metric_disconnect}

Offline evaluation systematically underestimates pruning-induced failure because per-step averages dilute catastrophic single-step events that individually terminate episodes.

X-VLA at $k{=}0.3$ demonstrates the gap directly: offline loss is 1.0014 (near-baseline), yet closed-loop SR drops to $22.0 \pm 0.9$\% ($-$77\,pp). A single gripper inversion (L2 $= 1.02$) is a minor perturbation in average action error but terminates an episode irreversibly. $\pi_0$'s offline loss at $k{=}0.1$ shows a 2.4\% \emph{improvement} over the unpruned baseline (offline metrics only; no closed-loop evaluation available), a result that closed-loop evaluation may contradict if similar catastrophic events occur at deployment frequency.

$\pi_0.5$ adds a distinct form of evidence: the 36\,pp gap between L2 and random removal at $k{=}0.5$ is invisible to any offline metric that averages across token subsets or selection methods. Offline evaluation would report degradation for both methods; only closed-loop evaluation reveals that the \emph{choice} of pruning strategy, not just the pruning ratio, determines whether the model remains functional.


\section{Confounder Analysis}
\label{app:confounders}

The seven models span three connector families but differ simultaneously in LLM backbone (PaliGemma for $\pi_0$/$\pi_0.5$, Llama-2 for OpenVLA, Qwen2 for SmolVLA), preventing clean isolation of the connector effect from backbone differences. The $\pi_0$--OpenVLA comparison (same 256 SigLIP-based tokens, different connector, backbone, and encoder fusion strategy) provides the closest available approximation, but a matched-backbone experiment remains the ideal control. This comparison involves differences in connector, backbone, and encoder fusion, limiting causal interpretation. Token count also varies across models (50 for X-VLA, 128 for SmolVLA, 256 for $\pi_0$/OpenVLA, 768 for $\pi_0.5$); the $\pi_0$--OpenVLA pair controls for this confounder within the CV analysis while still exhibiting the predicted CV-gap relationship.

The spatial clustering hypothesis for L2 failure on low-CV models is confirmed by token position analysis (Appendix~\ref{app:token_position}).

OpenVLA L2 and random results use different seeds (seed42 and seed7 respectively); same-seed (seed42) random replication confirms the cross-seed pattern at $k{=}0.3$ (21.4\% random vs.\ 25.0\% L2, gap 3.6\,pp) and $k{=}0.7$ (44.4\% random vs.\ 49.8\% L2, gap 5.4\,pp). Same-seed replication at $k{=}0.5$ was not completed within our evaluation budget; we report the cross-seed comparison in Table~\ref{tab:scaling_law} and defer same-seed confirmation to future work.


\section{Token Position Analysis}
\label{app:token_position}

To validate the spatial clustering hypothesis for L2 norm failure on low-CV models, we analyze the spatial distribution of tokens selected by L2 norm ranking on $\pi_0.5$ across three retention ratios.

At $k{=}0.7$ (538 of 768 tokens retained): DBSCAN clustering identifies 2 spatial clusters, with 97\% of retained tokens in the largest cluster and 100\% spatial coverage of the original image grid. The broad coverage explains why L2 selection still achieves 55.0\% SR at this retention ratio.

At $k{=}0.5$ (384 tokens retained): a single dominant cluster contains 100\% of retained tokens with only 78\% spatial coverage. The complete concentration into one spatial region confirms the clustering hypothesis: L2 norm ranking selects tokens from a contiguous high-norm region while abandoning 22\% of the spatial field. This is the retention ratio where L2 performance reaches its minimum (33.4\% SR, the V-shape valley).

At $k{=}0.3$ (230 tokens retained): 3 clusters emerge, with 86\% in the largest and 78\% spatial coverage. The fragmentation into multiple clusters at extreme compression is consistent with the partial L2 recovery observed at this ratio ($53.2 \pm 3.1$\% SR): when the budget is very small, L2 selection is forced to sample from multiple spatial regions, partially restoring coverage.

These results directly confirm that L2 norm ranking on low-CV models introduces spatial bias that degrades performance, and that the severity peaks at intermediate retention ratios where concentration is maximal.


\section{CV-Strategy Interaction at Extreme Compression}
\label{app:cv_strategy_interaction}

